<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  goes in res/mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml AND ic_launcher_round.xml

  TRANSPARENT background, by request.

  An adaptive icon still needs a background LAYER - the format requires one - but the layer
  may be fully transparent, and then the launcher composites the mark straight onto the
  wallpaper instead of onto a tile. That is the look: a folder mark, not a badge.

  Two consequences worth knowing rather than rediscovering:

  * Launchers that force a shape (Pixel's circle, several OEM skins) mask a transparent
    background to nothing visible, so the mark floats. That is the intent here.
  * The foreground's own 72x72 safe zone still applies. Nothing moved, so no mask can crop
    the mark - see the comment in ic_launcher_foreground.xml.

  The monochrome layer is unaffected: themed icons draw the silhouette on the launcher's own
  background regardless of what this layer says.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@android:color/transparent" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
